tests/full/obp -- the OBP (openboot.bin) equivalence gate
=========================================================

Proves the GNU/Linux build of openboot.bin (build/obp/gnu/) is equivalent to
Sun's own Solaris/Sun-Studio build from the same source. The full claim,
method and evidence are in TESTS; this file is only the map of what runs
where.

    make obp-verify            the internal gate (below), also `make obp-test`,
                               same thing
    make obp-test-external TARBALL=<tarball>
                               the external checks (below)

Layout
------
    run           runs every internal/*.sh in order, stops at the first
                  failure; shares one pci build across the ones that need it
                  (lib/build-pci.sh)
    internal/     one script per check, no tarball needed -- everything they
                  read is already in this repository (src/obp,
                  PRISTINE-MANIFEST, expected/*.sha256)
    external/     needs OpenSPARCT2_Arch.1.2.tar.bz2 (OPENSPARC_T2=<path>, or
                  pass the path directly). Confirms the internal checks'
                  assumptions against Sun's actual release archive, and is
                  how expected/*.sha256 get regenerated after a src/obp
                  change. Not run by `run` or `make obp-verify`.
    lib/          shared code: dic-diff.py and records.py (the OBMD/dic
                  comparison logic every test above builds on), dict-dump.sh,
                  compare-reference.sh, decomp-ab/ (Appendix A tools),
                  build-pci.sh (the shared-build helper). The decomp-ab tools
                  are those of Appendix A of TESTS: they compare, byte for
                  byte, a build by the GNU path with one by Sun's own
                  toolchain; they take their directories as arguments. The
                  Sun-built capture they were run against is not in this
                  repository; the gate does not use it (its pinned hashes are
                  in expected/sun-records.sha256).
    expected/     committed hashes an internal test checks against --
                  sun-records.sha256, dict-dump.sha256. Never edit by hand;
                  external/sun-records-regenerate.sh writes both.
                  dic-user-area.<name>.txt are not hashes: the user-area cells
                  that differ from Sun's host dictionary and that no
                  correction (entry delta, length shift) explains (dic-host;
                  dic-diff.py OURS SUNS --write-user-pin FILE writes one, to
                  be reviewed before it is committed).
    TESTS         the full narrative: the claim, every test's method and
                  measured result, the differences that remain and why each
                  is not a defect, the whole build-layer inventory.

Build-time tools live under build/obp/gnu/tools/ instead (forth32.sh,
forth64.sh, makedi.sh, makeprom.sh, bootstrap-dics.sh, decomp-size.sh) --
obp-gnu.mk depends on them to produce openboot.bin itself, not just to test
it, so they stay next to the build they serve.

Internal tests, in the order `run` uses
----------------------------------------
    src-intact       PRISTINE-MANIFEST vs the working tree (whole repo, not
                     just OBP)
    dic-kernels      the bootstrapped kernel dictionaries vs src/obp
    dic-fixpoint     a second bootstrap generation from our own root
                     reproduces itself
    dic-host         the host dictionaries vs src/obp, reconciled for the
                     known differences (dic-diff.py); the user-area cells that
                     no correction explains are pinned (expected/dic-user-area.*)
    sun-records      pristine builds vs Sun's own shipped images
    patch-locality   patched vs pristine: only the patched dropins differ
    dict-content     the product's dictionary word list (regression check)

External tests
--------------
    src-pristine              PRISTINE-MANIFEST vs the tarball itself, not
                              just the working tree
    sun-records-regenerate    rewrites expected/*.sha256 from the tarball
                              and a fresh build; review the diff before
                              committing

Every script takes QEMU_SPARC32 and QEMU_SPARC64 (a qemu-sparc32plus and a
qemu-sparc64 linux-user binary) from the environment; `make` sets both from
config.mk. Running a script directly needs them set by hand -- see
REQUIREMENTS.LINUX.
